// Walkthrough 2 - the subscriber, BUILT rather than read. Level 1: every line
// given and explained. This is the alternative road: the Inspector list on
// CaveSwitch already works, and this is how you would do the same job in code -
// which is what you need the moment the thing you want to trigger is not a
// listener somebody else already wired. It also plants 5.3: a listener that
// outlives its object has to be taken off the event.
const WALK_SUBSCRIBE = {
  ask: 'how do I make my own object open the door when the lever is pulled?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/DoorOpener.cs (plus one line of Lever.cs)',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro:
    'The Inspector list on `CaveSwitch` already opens the cave door — that is the road you just used, and it needs no code and no edits to anybody else\'s file. This walkthrough builds the same connection in a script of your own, which is the road you take when the thing you want to happen is not already a listener. Both roads end at the same door.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Open the event up - the one edit to somebody else\'s file',
      lead: 'Read the two lines at the top of `Lever.cs` again. The events are `[SerializeField] private` — and that one word, `private`, is the whole reason the Inspector was the only road available.',
      lines: [
        {
          code: '    // BEFORE:  [SerializeField] private UnityEvent onLeverOn;',
          what: 'A comment, so you can see what you are changing. Nothing else in `Lever.cs` moves today: not `Interact()`, not the sprite swap, not the `Invoke()`.',
        },
        {
          code: '    public UnityEvent onLeverOn;',
          what: 'The same field with **one word changed**: delete `private` and the `[SerializeField]` in front of it, and write `public`.',
