          code: '        lever.onLeverOn.RemoveListener(OpenDoor);',
          what: 'Takes `OpenDoor` back off the event. Same three pieces as the `AddListener(...)` line, with the same method, in the same order.',
          why: '**A listener that outlives its object is a call into something that is no longer there.** The event is holding a reference to **your object**, and if you are destroyed while still subscribed, the next pull of the lever calls a method on a destroyed object and Unity throws a `MissingReferenceException`. **You will meet this properly in 5.3**, when objects start outliving scenes. This two-line pair is the smallest version of being careful.',
        },
        {
          code: '    }',
          what: '',
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.Events;

// DoorOpener.cs - this script's whole job is to be listening when the lever is
// pulled. It never touches Lever.cs at runtime: the lever calls OpenDoor without
// knowing this file exists, and it would call anything else the same way.
public class DoorOpener : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private Lever lever;        // the lever this door listens to
    [SerializeField] private GameObject door;    // the thing that disappears

    // The same method, kept as a value. AddListener and RemoveListener have to
    // be handed the SAME delegate, and holding it in a field is what makes the
    // removal exact rather than hopeful - and it is the only way once there is
    // more than one method to take off.
    private UnityAction onLeverPulled;

    private void Start()
    {
        onLeverPulled = OpenDoor;
        lever.onLeverOn.AddListener(onLeverPulled);
    }

    private void OnDestroy()
    {
        // The pair to Start(). A listener that outlives its object is a call
        // into something that is no longer there.
        lever.onLeverOn.RemoveListener(onLeverPulled);
    }

    private void OpenDoor()
    {
        door.SetActive(false);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. Three things carry the lesson, and all three have to match: the access level you changed on `onLeverOn`, the subscription in `Start()`, and the unsubscription in `OnDestroy()`. This version keeps the method in a `UnityAction` field and hands the same value to both calls, where the walkthrough passed the method group straight into each one — the walkthrough\'s form is correct and shorter, and this is the form you reach for once there are several methods to remove.',
};

const body = `<h1>5.2 — The Door: Opening It with Events</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have an axe, and the cave door does not care — yet. Today you close the last gap: press
    <strong>E</strong> beside the lever, and a door the lever has never met swings open. The lever
    knows nothing about doors. It flips a sprite and it fires an <strong>event</strong>, and who is
    listening is somebody else's business. That gap between cause and effect is called
    <em>abstraction</em>, and it is the most exam-relevant idea in this whole project.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I open a door with a key?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('5.2', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts/Lever.cs</code> — the one script
            in that folder which is <strong>not</strong> an empty shell. Read the whole thing; it is
            short. Two fields at the top are <code>UnityEvent</code>s, and the word in front of both
            is <code>private</code>. Then notice what is missing: the word "door" appears nowhere in
            that file. The lever has no idea what it is switching on.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Overworld → CaveSwitch</strong> — the object standing in the
            <em>scene</em>, not the prefab in the Project window — then
            <strong>Inspector → On Lever On</strong>. Somebody has already wired it up: three
            listeners, each one calling <code>SetActive</code> with a true/false argument. Those
            listeners are <em>prefab-instance overrides</em>, so the prefab asset's own lists are
            still empty — inspecting the prefab and seeing nothing wired is not a bug.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → expand the first two listeners</strong>: they target
            <code>Collision Door Removed</code> and <code>No Collision Door Removed</code>. Find
            both in the <strong>Hierarchy</strong> and look at them in the <strong>Scene view</strong>.
            Together they <em>are</em> the door — one object for the wall you can feel, one for the
            wall you can see, switched off at the same moment. <strong>The door mechanism already
            works.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → expand the third listener</strong>: it points at the scene's
            <code>Canvas</code>, which nothing in this scene uses yet, with no note explaining why
            the lever turns it off. That is your first piece of genuine legacy wiring. Then expand
            <strong>On Lever Off</strong>: it is <em>empty</em>. (If the list shows five rows rather
            than three, two are leftover blank entries from when it was made shorter — they target
            nothing and never fire.)
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play, walk to the lever, and press <strong>E</strong>.
            <em>Nothing happens.</em> The lever is fully wired to the door, and no line in your
            controller has ever asked about the E key — that is the half you are about to write.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once both walkthroughs are done — Game view →</strong> Play, walk to the lever,
            press E. The wall vanishes: a door somebody else built, opened by the interaction you
            wrote, with nothing about the door in your code and nothing about your code in the door.
            That is the whole lesson in one key press.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → On Lever On →</strong> add a fourth listener of your own choosing —
            reveal a message, swap a sprite, disable a hazard. Everything that list can call is a
            public method or property on some component, which is the same constraint your own
            scripts live under: <strong>zero lines of code, and the game has a new rule.</strong>
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every row below is a call you will type in the next two walkthroughs. Four are calls you
        have met in another lesson; two are the new mechanism.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.E)</code></td>
            <td>True on the <strong>single frame</strong> the key goes down — the moment rather than
                the state, and the keyboard twin of the <code>GetMouseButtonDown</code> behind your
                attack in <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a>.</td>
            <td>Choosing the wrong twin is invisible in the code and obvious in the game:
                <code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.E)</code> is true on every frame the key is held, so one
                press becomes sixty.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Object.FindFirstObjectByType&lt;Lever&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>A scene-wide search that hands back the first <code>Lever</code> component, without
                you ever holding a reference to it. The Unity 6 spelling — the old
                <code>FindObjectOfType&lt;T&gt;()</code> is deprecated.</td>
            <td>It searches the whole scene: fine once per key press, wasteful once per frame. And
                it hands back a <strong>component</strong>, never a GameObject.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code></td>
            <td>The straight-line distance between two points — the call that decided whether the
                turret could see you in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>, now deciding whether you can
                reach the lever.</td>
            <td>It measures <em>between two positions</em>, so both have to exist. Ask it about a
                <code>null</code> reference and the script stops, every frame, pointing at that
                line.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>lever.transform</code></td>
            <td>A component's shortcut to the Transform of the object it is attached to — how a
                search that returns a <em>component</em> still gives you a <em>position</em>.</td>
            <td>Nothing. A component has no position of its own; it always reaches through its own
                object.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>lever.Interact()</code> — and the word <code>public</code></td>
            <td>The lever's one offered method. The player contributes a moment in time; the lever
                does everything else.</td>
            <td>It has to be <code>public</code>. A <code>private</code> method is visible only
                inside its own class, so the compiler refuses the call outright — error CS0122.
                That is the difference between a method that is used and one that is
                <em>offered</em>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>onLeverOn.Invoke()</code><br>
                <code>onLeverOn.AddListener(m)</code><br>
                <code>onLeverOn.RemoveListener(m)</code></td>
            <td>The cause, and the two ways to attach an effect. <code>Invoke()</code> runs every
                method that has subscribed and names not one of them; <code>AddListener(m)</code>
                hands your method over as a <em>value</em>, with no parentheses on it.</td>
            <td>The event accepts only methods that take nothing and return nothing, so the shape of
                your handler is not yours to choose. And a listener you never remove outlives its
                object — the next <code>Invoke()</code> calls into something that has been
                destroyed.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification — the interaction you built:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The moment.</strong> Which input call fires on a single frame, and what does
                the lever do if you use the one that fires on every frame instead?</li>
            <li><strong>The search.</strong> What does the search hand back — a component or a
                GameObject — and what does it hand back in a scene with no lever in it? What happens
                on the next line if you do not check for that?</li>
            <li><strong>The reach.</strong> What does "close enough" mean numerically, where does
                that number live so a designer can change it, and which comparison operator did you
                pick at the boundary?</li>
            <li><strong>The call.</strong> Why must <code>Interact()</code> be <code>public</code>
                for your line to compile — and what does your own script know about the lever's
                state after it calls it?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Where does the game's knowledge live?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The lever opens a door it has never met. Which window do you open to teach it about the door — and what does your answer say about where the game's rules actually live?</li>
            <li>If the lever had been written the obvious way — holding a reference to the door and calling <code>door.Open()</code> directly — what would you have to change to make a second lever open a second door? What does the event version cost instead?</li>
            <li>The exam calls a procedure that calls other procedures without knowing their names <strong>abstraction</strong>. What would the lever have needed to know without UnityEvent, and what complexity does the event remove?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

